1	# Custom Notification Stack -- Implementation Plan
2	
3	## 0. Decision: sonner stays installed, but fully unmounted from the app
4	
5	Remove the `<Toaster>` mount and the `sonner` import from `src/App.tsx`. Do NOT
6	touch `src/components/ui/sonner.tsx` (CLAUDE.md forbids editing generated `ui/*`
7	files) and do NOT run `npm uninstall sonner` -- leave the dependency in
8	`package.json` untouched. Rationale: `ui/sonner.tsx` is shadcn-generated and
9	off-limits either way; removing the npm dependency is a separate, riskier,
10	unnecessary step (could affect lockfile / future shadcn regen) for zero
11	runtime benefit once nothing imports it. Net effect: sonner code is simply dead
12	weight in `node_modules`, nothing imports `sonner` or `ui/sonner.tsx` anymore.
13	
14	## 1. New files
15	
16	- `src/utils/notificationStore.ts` -- plain module-level store (no state lib),
17	  mirrors the module-state + subscriber pattern already used in this repo
18	  (e.g. how local hooks like `use-gcode-preview.ts` manage state without a
19	  store lib). Kept as a direct import between `toast.ts` and
20	  `NotificationStack.tsx` since they are tightly coupled 1:1 -- no need to
21	  round-trip through `window` events for this internal wiring; `window`
22	  events stay reserved for the existing cross-component signal bus per
23	  CLAUDE.md convention.
24	- `src/components/NotificationStack.tsx` -- the stack UI, mounted once in
25	  `App.tsx` in place of `<Toaster>`.
26	- `src/components/NotificationStack.module.scss` -- glass styling, following
27	  the `.bar` / `.toolbar` recipe from GCodePreviewBar / SliceSidebar.
28	
29	`src/utils/toast.ts` is edited (not replaced) to call into the new store
30	instead of `sonner`, keeping exact existing exported signatures.
31	
32	## 2. Store shape (`src/utils/notificationStore.ts`)
33	
34	```ts
35	export type NotificationVariant = 'success' | 'error' | 'warning' | 'loading';
36	
37	export interface NotificationItem {
38	  id: string;
39	  variant: NotificationVariant;
40	  message: string;
41	  createdAt: number; // Date.now(), used only for stable ordering
42	}
43	
44	type Listener = (items: NotificationItem[], frontId: string | null) => void;
45	
46	let items: NotificationItem[] = [];
47	// id of the notification currently pinned to front by the wheel gesture (or
48	// by having just been pushed). null = no override -> selector falls back to
49	// most recent item (see NotificationStack's orderedIds derivation).
50	let frontId: string | null = null;
51	const listeners = new Set<Listener>();
52	
53	function emit() {
54	  const snapshot = items.slice();
55	  listeners.forEach((l) => l(snapshot, frontId));
56	}
57	
58	let uid = 0;
59	function genId() {
60	  uid += 1;
61	  return `ntf-${Date.now()}-${uid}`;
62	}
63	
64	export function pushNotification(variant: NotificationVariant, message: string): string {
65	  const id = genId();
66	  items = [...items, { id, variant, message, createdAt: Date.now() }];
67	  frontId = id; // a new notification always becomes the front card
68	  emit();
69	  return id;
70	}
71	
72	/** Used by showLoading's returned id + dismissToast, and by the (x) button. */
73	export function removeNotification(id: string): void {
74	  const idx = items.findIndex((n) => n.id === id);
75	  if (idx === -1) return;
76	  items = items.filter((n) => n.id !== id);
77	  if (frontId === id) frontId = null; // stack re-derives front (see selector)
78	  emit();
79	}
80	
81	/** Called by NotificationStack's wheel handler. */
82	export function setFrontNotification(id: string): void {
83	  if (!items.some((n) => n.id === id)) return;
84	  frontId = id;
85	  emit();
86	}
87	
88	export function subscribeToNotifications(listener: Listener): () => void {
89	  listeners.add(listener);
90	  listener(items.slice(), frontId);
91	  return () => listeners.delete(listener);
92	}
93	```
94	
95	Notes:
96	- The `loading` variant is kept only because `showLoading` / `dismissToast`
97	  must keep working (they currently have no call sites in the reviewed files,
98	  but are part of the public `toast.ts` API and must not regress). It renders
99	  like the others -- no spinner infra needed since these permanent cards have
100	  no timer to race against; a static "loading" tint on the icon is enough.
101	- Ordering is push order (`items`, oldest first, newest last). The stack
102	  always visually derives "who's on top" from `frontId ?? items[items.length-1]?.id`,
103	  so a fresh `pushNotification` always naturally becomes front.
104	
105	## 3. `src/utils/toast.ts` (edited, same exported signatures)
106	
107	```ts
108	import { pushNotification, removeNotification } from './notificationStore';
109	
110	export const showSuccess = (message: string) => {
111	  pushNotification('success', message);
112	};
113	
114	export const showError = (message: string) => {
115	  pushNotification('error', message);
116	};
117	
118	export const showWarning = (message: string) => {
119	  pushNotification('warning', message);
120	};
121	
122	export const showLoading = (message: string) => {
123	  return pushNotification('loading', message);
124	};
125	
126	export const dismissToast = (toastId: string) => {
127	  removeNotification(toastId);
128	};
129	```
130	
131	All ~25 existing call sites (AxisSidebar.tsx, use-gcode-preview.ts,
132	CategoriesSidebar.tsx, Index.tsx, ModelingSidebar.tsx,
133	PrinterConfigSidebar.tsx, TextureEditorModal.tsx, Viewport3D.tsx,
134	ViewCube.tsx) need ZERO changes -- they only import
135	`showSuccess`/`showError`/`showWarning`/`showLoading`/`dismissToast` from
136	`@/utils/toast`, whose signatures are unchanged.
137	
138	## 4. `src/components/NotificationStack.tsx`
139	
140	### Responsibilities
141	- Subscribe to `notificationStore` for `(items, frontId)`.
142	- Render the stack container, anchored bottom-right identically to the old
143	  sonner mount: same `right: 24px` / `bottom: 84px` offset documented in
144	  `App.tsx`'s old comment, reimplemented as fixed positioning in the SCSS
145	  module.
146	- Compute per-card stack position (`stackIndex`, 0 = front) and render only a
147	  capped number of "peek" cards behind the front one.
148	- Attach an `onWheel` handler on the stack container.
149	- Render nothing (`return null`) when `items.length === 0`.
150	
151	### State shape inside the component
152	
153	```tsx
154	const [items, setItems] = useState<NotificationItem[]>([]);
155	const [frontId, setFrontIdState] = useState<string | null>(null);
156	
157	useEffect(() => subscribeToNotifications((its, front) => {
158	  setItems(its);
159	  setFrontIdState(front);
160	}), []);
161	```
162	
163	### Deriving render order (front -> back)
164	
165	```ts
166	const MAX_PEEK = 3; // front + up to 3 peeking behind = 4 cards max rendered
167	
168	const orderedIds = useMemo(() => {
169	  if (items.length === 0) return [];
170	  const ids = items.map((n) => n.id);
171	  const resolvedFront = frontId && ids.includes(frontId) ? frontId : ids[ids.length - 1];
172	  // Everything else, newest-first, forms the rest of the cycle order.
173	  const rest = ids.filter((id) => id !== resolvedFront).reverse();
174	  return [resolvedFront, ...rest];
175	}, [items, frontId]);
176	
177	const visibleIds = orderedIds.slice(0, MAX_PEEK + 1); // front + up to 3 peeking
178	```
179	
180	Each id in `visibleIds` gets `stackIndex` = its position in that array (0 =
181	front, fully drawn/interactive; 1..3 = peeking slivers). DECISION: peeking
182	cards are not interactive at all -- only the front card has a live (x) and
183	receives wheel; to close a specific non-front notification the user must
184	first scroll it to front. This keeps hit-testing simple and matches the
185	"stacked deck, wheel brings one to front" mental model.
186	
187	Cards beyond `MAX_PEEK+1` are not rendered (DOM stays small) but still exist
188	in `items` and become visible again as they cycle to a top-4 slot via wheel.
189	
190	### Per-card transform (the "peek" visual)
191	
192	```ts
193	const PEEK_OFFSET_X = 10; // px per layer back, rightward
194	const PEEK_OFFSET_Y = -10; // px per layer back, upward (matches reference: top/right edges peek out)
195	const SCALE_STEP = 0.05;
196	const OPACITY_STEP = 0.18;
197	
198	// for stackIndex i in 0..3:
199	transform: `translate(${i * PEEK_OFFSET_X}px, ${i * PEEK_OFFSET_Y}px) scale(${1 - i * SCALE_STEP})`,
200	opacity: Math.max(0.15, 1 - i * OPACITY_STEP),
201	zIndex: 100 - i,
202	```
203	
204	Card 0 = full size/opacity, frontmost. Cards 1-3 progressively smaller, more
205	transparent, offset up-and-right, and painted behind (lower zIndex) so the
206	front card naturally covers most of their area except the intended peek
207	sliver.
208	
209	Animate with `framer-motion` (`motion.div`, already a project dependency)
210	rather than raw CSS transitions, because:
211	- Cards need `layout`-aware animation when the stack reflows (item removed
212	  from the middle, or wheel changes front) -- `AnimatePresence` + per-card
213	  `animate` targets handle this cleanly across React key-based reorders.
214	- Use `<AnimatePresence mode="popLayout">` wrapping the mapped cards, each
215	  `motion.div` keyed by `item.id`:
216	  - `initial`: `{ opacity: 0, y: 20, scale: 0.9 }` (new card slides up from
217	    below the stack)
218	  - `animate`: the computed `{ x, y, scale, opacity }` for its current
219	    `stackIndex` (framer interpolates automatically on index change -- this
220	    is what produces the fluid wheel-driven slide, see section 5)
221	  - `exit`: `{ opacity: 0, x: 40, scale: 0.85, transition: { duration: 0.18 } }`
222	    (close (x) slides the card out to the right and fades)
223	  - `transition`: `{ type: 'spring', stiffness: 380, damping: 34 }` for
224	    stack-position changes.
225	
226	### JSX sketch
227	
228	```tsx
229	<div className={styles.stack} onWheel={handleWheel}>
230	  <AnimatePresence mode="popLayout">
231	    {visibleIds.map((id, stackIndex) => {
232	      const item = items.find((n) => n.id === id)!;
233	      return (
234	        <motion.div
235	          key={id}
236	          className={styles.card}
237	          data-variant={item.variant}
238	          style={{ zIndex: 100 - stackIndex }}
239	          initial={{ opacity: 0, y: 20, scale: 0.9 }}
240	          animate={{
241	            x: stackIndex * PEEK_OFFSET_X,
242	            y: stackIndex * PEEK_OFFSET_Y,
243	            scale: 1 - stackIndex * SCALE_STEP,
244	            opacity: Math.max(0.15, 1 - stackIndex * OPACITY_STEP),
245	          }}
246	          exit={{ opacity: 0, x: 40, scale: 0.85 }}
247	          transition={{ type: 'spring', stiffness: 380, damping: 34 }}
248	        >
249	          {stackIndex === 0 && (
250	            <>
251	              <NotificationIcon variant={item.variant} />
252	              <span className={styles.message}>{item.message}</span>
253	              <button
254	                className={styles.close}
255	                aria-label="Fermer"
256	                onClick={() => removeNotification(id)}
257	              >
258	                <X size={14} />
259	              </button>
260	            </>
261	          )}
262	          {stackIndex > 0 && <div className={styles.peekFill} />}
263	        </motion.div>
264	      );
265	    })}
266	  </AnimatePresence>
267	</div>
268	```
269	
270	(`peekFill` = an empty opaque-tinted glass rectangle for non-front cards --
271	no need to render icon/text/close for cards the user can't read anyway since
272	they are mostly hidden and scaled/faded down; this also avoids any
273	accessibility noise from off-screen-ish duplicate text.)
274	
275	## 5. Wheel-cycling logic (`handleWheel`)
276	
277	Interpretation chosen: scrolling down (`deltaY > 0`) cycles to the next card
278	further back in the current visual stack (the current `stackIndex 1` card
279	becomes front); scrolling up (`deltaY < 0`) cycles the other direction,
280	bringing back the previously-front card. This matches the "scroll down = go
281	forward/deeper" convention used elsewhere (e.g. carousels) and reverses
282	cleanly on scroll-up.
283	
284	```ts
285	const wheelLockRef = useRef(false);
286	
287	const handleWheel = (e: React.WheelEvent) => {
288	  if (orderedIds.length < 2) return;
289	  e.preventDefault(); // fixed-position overlay: never let this scroll page content behind it
290	  if (wheelLockRef.current) return; // one wheel "tick" = one step, not several
291	  wheelLockRef.current = true;
292	  setTimeout(() => { wheelLockRef.current = false; }, 220); // ~ spring settle time
293	  const delta = e.deltaY > 0 ? 1 : -1;
294	  const nextIdx = ((delta % orderedIds.length) + orderedIds.length) % orderedIds.length;
295	  setFrontNotification(orderedIds[nextIdx]);
296	};
297	```
298	
299	Because `orderedIds` is recomputed from `frontId` + recency every render,
300	calling `setFrontNotification(nextId)` triggers a store emit -> re-render ->
301	`orderedIds` reshuffles so `nextId` is now at index 0 -> every card's
302	`stackIndex` (and therefore its `animate` target) changes -> framer-motion's
303	spring transition animates each card smoothly from its old slot to its new
304	slot. This produces "la molette les fait glisser dans une animation fluide"
305	with no manual animation code beyond `animate` already being driven by
306	`stackIndex`.
307	
308	### Interaction with new arrivals while a mid-stack item is focused to front
309	
310	Per section 2/3, `pushNotification` unconditionally sets `frontId = <new id>`.
311	A new notification always interrupts and takes over the front slot, even if
312	the user had manually scrolled an older one to front. This is the simplest,
313	most predictable rule (new events are the most recent/important thing
314	happening) and matches how the old sonner stack behaved (new toast always
315	appears at the top position). The previously-front (user-pinned) card
316	animates back into the peek stack via the same `stackIndex` recompute +
317	spring, exactly like any other reordering.
318	
319	## 6. Close (x) button behavior
320	
321	`onClick={() => removeNotification(id)}` on the front card only (section 4
322	decision). `removeNotification`:
323	- Splices the item out of `items`.
324	- If the removed id was `frontId`, resets `frontId = null` so the selector
325	  falls back to "most recent remaining item" as the new front.
326	- Triggers `AnimatePresence`'s `exit` animation (slide right + fade) for the
327	  removed card while every remaining card's `stackIndex` shifts down by one
328	  and re-animates into its new slot via the same spring -- a natural "stack
329	  reflow".
330	
331	## 7. Blur-without-ghosting -- resolution of the open question
332	
333	CONCLUSION: no special stacking-context/isolation trickery is required.
334	`backdrop-filter` only samples pixels already painted behind the element
335	within the same compositing context, at the moment of paint. Because:
336	- Every card (front and peeking) is rendered with a translucent glass
337	  background (`rgba(10, 15, 26, 0.25)`, matching the existing recipe -- not
338	  fully opaque, by design), and
339	- Cards behind the front one are painted first (lower `zIndex`) and are
340	  positioned so the front card covers their bounding box except for the
341	  small peek slivers,
342	
343	...the front card's `backdrop-filter: blur(4px)` correctly samples: (a) the
344	3D viewport wherever no card is behind it, and (b) the peeking cards' own
345	glass fill wherever they stick out from behind the front card. That second
346	case is NOT "ghosting" -- it is the intended visual (the peek slivers are a
347	deliberate part of the stacked-deck look). The real risk the task is
348	flagging -- peeking cards' TEXT/icons showing blurred through the front card
349	-- is structurally impossible here because ONLY the front card renders
350	text/icon content (section 4, `stackIndex === 0 &&` guard); peeking cards
351	render an empty `.peekFill` div, so there is no content behind the front card
352	that could "ghost" through the blur beyond the flat glass-tinted rectangles,
353	which is the desired peek effect itself.
354	
355	The one real thing to add to the SCSS: give `.stack` `isolation: isolate;`
356	-- not to fix ghosting (there is none, per above), but purely for
357	defensive consistency with the existing codebase convention
358	(`GCodePreviewBar.module.scss`'s `.bar` also sets `isolation: isolate;`),
359	so the stack's z-index layering never interacts unpredictably with sibling
360	fixed-position chrome (SliceSidebar toolbar, GCodePreviewBar) that also
361	creates stacking contexts.
362	
363	## 8. SCSS (`src/components/NotificationStack.module.scss`)
364	
365	Follow the exact glass recipe from `GCodePreviewBar.module.scss` /
366	`SliceSidebar.module.scss`:
367	
368	```scss
369	.stack {
370	  position: fixed;
371	  right: 24px;
372	  bottom: 84px;
373	  width: 0;
374	  height: 0;
375	  z-index: 60; // above viewport chrome, consistent with other floating panels
376	  isolation: isolate;
377	  pointer-events: none; // re-enabled per-card below so empty space isn't a hit target
378	}
379	
380	.card {
381	  position: absolute;
382	  right: 0;
383	  bottom: 0;
384	  min-width: 280px;
385	  max-width: 360px;
386	  display: flex;
387	  align-items: center;
388	  gap: 10px;
389	  padding: 12px 14px;
390	  border-radius: 14px;
391	  border: none;
392	  box-shadow: 0 8px 32px 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4), 0 2px 8px 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3);
393	  background: rgba(10, 15, 26, 0.25);
394	  backdrop-filter: blur(4px);
395	  -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(4px);
396	  color: rgb(212, 212, 216);
397	  pointer-events: auto;
398	
399	  &:not(:first-child) {
400	    pointer-events: none; // peeking cards are inert, see sections 4/5
401	  }
402	}
403	
404	.peekFill {
405	  width: 100%;
406	  height: 100%;
407	  border-radius: inherit;
408	}
409	
410	.message {
411	  flex: 1;
412	  font-size: 13px;
413	  line-height: 1.4;
414	}
415	
416	.close {
417	  flex: 0 0 auto;
418	  display: flex;
419	  align-items: center;
420	  justify-content: center;
421	  width: 22px;
422	  height: 22px;
423	  border-radius: 6px;
424	  background: transparent;
425	  border: none;
426	  color: rgba(212, 212, 216, 0.7);
427	  cursor: pointer;
428	  &:hover { background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); color: rgb(212, 212, 216); }
429	}
430	
431	// Variant accent (left icon color only -- keep the glass bg identical across
432	// variants per "matching the rest of the chrome", don't reintroduce sonner's
433	// colored-background style):
